1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:13,704
95 to 100 percent: the share of homes heating mainly with oil, propane or electricity, and already
running air conditioning, that a national study estimates would pay lower bills with a heat pump.

2
00:00:14,233 --> 00:00:17,809
The Commission has looked at what
that figure is, and what it is not.

3
00:00:18,333 --> 00:00:27,981
It is a model: the study simulated the bills of such homes across the country, so
the figure is an estimate of what bills would be, not a reading from anyone's meter.

4
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:39,972
The range moves with the heat pump's efficiency, and so does the saving: the
median it estimated was $300 to $650 a year, for those same homes and no others.

5
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:51,680
The air conditioning is not a detail: in the same model, homes on those
fuels without it do worse, with only 73 to 86 percent coming out ahead.

6
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:56,996
A model, the Commission notes,
is exact about what it assumes.

7
00:00:57,300 --> 00:01:08,004
Whether metered homes in Minnesota and New England agree with the model, and whether oil
holds the pattern, is the subject of the full inquiry, linked in the description below.
